  "raw": " catch up, aside from fixing flaws in the first iterative release of the Galaxy Alpha handset, Samsung noticed that 25 million phones — not just Galaxy Alphas — were affected by a software bug that lowered their performance as they bumped against dust and other debris, according to Reuters. You might be thinking: Who is this small- or medium-sized markets that no maker wants, aside from Chinese manufacturers? Well, in September 2013, Google shut down Motorola Mobility's pay-as-you-go service Moto Maker (no longer supported), which let consumers design their own phones, in part due to failing sales. This also formed Samsung's justification in discontinuing the price-constrained high-end Galaxy Expert device last year; over half of its volume has reportedly come… [Read full story]",
